# Implement fast and final CPU quality profiles

## Scope and existing evidence

Historical implementation evidence covers typed structures only, unless
explicitly stated otherwise; the finite contract is not rendered output.

Use the same vectors, seeds, blend order, and page geometry while varying only
declared texture resolution and sampling cost.

Current executable evidence now makes Preview and Final explicit quality roles.
A pair validates only when vector geometry, calibrated material authority,
physical noise scale, render seed, material blend order, and physical bounds
remain exactly shared; caller-owned texture resolution and sampling cost may
differ. Successful comparison now returns constructor-sealed evidence borrowing
the exact preview and final profiles.

An exhaustive 256-mask oracle crosses both wrong-role states with independent
drift in all six shared-authority fields. It requires direct and sealed
admission to preserve preview-role, final-role, then authority-mismatch
precedence while quality-only costs differ. Rendering, concrete quality values,
performance budgets, scheduling, and sampling algorithms remain open.

Current design evidence freezes Render as a read-only application capability
bound to one accepted revision and deterministic vector/material inputs. Preview
and final profiles share geometry, seeds, physical dimensions, and blend order;
quality-only sampling may differ. Render identity, retry safety, invalidation,
read-only cancellation/progress semantics, typed projection results, no-file
behavior, and browser/CLI/MCP parity are frozen while implementation remains
open.

## Finite CPU Preview and Final rendering cost contract

**Inputs:** one accepted vector page, immutable material authority,
physical bounds, document seed, exact material blend order and physical
texture scale. **Outputs:** one canonical `Preview` and one `Final` render
request with identical authoritative input and different declared pixel/
sampling costs, or a shared-authority refusal. No compute GPU is required.

**Fixed first-release profiles (proposed):** render one A4 page of
`210 x 297 mm`. `Preview` samples at 96 DPI with one sample per pixel;
`Final` samples at 300 DPI with four color samples per output pixel.
Both require CPU tiling at most `256 x 256` pixels per processing tile and
a capped working-memory target of 256 MiB for one page. On any resource
exhaustion they refuse the requested quality instead of falling back to an
unreported lower DPI or changed page geometry.

**Shared authority:** profile comparison requires exact equality of vector
geometry identity, material/contact calibration version, accepted page
bounds, 4 mm paper-noise scale, document seed and layer ordering. Only
texture resolution/sampling cost may differ. Equation shape, table rules,
handwriting joins, line art and clipping must therefore be identical in
physical coordinates; raster edge softness can differ as sampling quality.

**Worked example:** revision `r9` requests the same diagram and `t1` stroke
under `Preview` at 96 DPI, then `Final` at 300 DPI. The stroke endpoints
remain `(20,30)` and `(52,30)` mm, its material key remains `seed-4/t1`,
and only output pixel density and sample count vary. Identical source inputs
must replay identical material noise in page coordinates. No different font
or missing ink may be inserted solely to meet Preview latency.

**Refusal example:** a Final request reorders ink layers, changes stroke
bounds by 0.1 mm, requests a new seed or exceeds the memory budget. Reject
quality-pair admission or resource scheduling without publishing partial
output as a valid Final page. Render is read-only: these requests cannot
save a PDF, write an image path, or arm/start a machine without a distinct
approved Export or physical operation.

**Assumptions and limits:** typed Preview/Final profiles and exact shared-
authority checks already exist; concrete DPI, tile/memory policies, CPU
rasterization, progress, cancellation and output serialization do not.
This finite configuration is a design target, not an observed latency result
or certified identical raster pixels across machines.

## Acceptance decision

- **Basis:** practical for Preview/Final role and authority comparison;
  theoretical for CPU raster costs and scheduling, 2026-11-02.
- **Delivered:** two explicit DPI/sample profiles, 256 MiB bound, one
  reproducibility example and drift/resource refusals.
- **Not implemented by this acceptance:** production CPU raster engine,
  benchmarked latency, memory enforcement or downloadable render output.
